this is a production of Cornell  University  well thank you all for being here today  I'm excited to talk about what I've been  doing the past two years it's definitely  flown by so just as an introduction to  this topic Don mentioned that I had  spent some time at the Birmingham  botanical gardens and working in their  native plant collection I really develop  some interests that were instrumental in  developing this topic public gardens  addressing environmental issues through  the use of native plants  so native plants have existed for  millennia have evolved with their  environments and have become  increasingly popular in the cultivated  landscape for the purposes of this  research a native plant is one that  occurs naturally in a particular region  state ecosystem inhabit without direct  or indirect human action and to provide  a context for this study I am studying  native plants in public gardens which  are living museums mission based  institutions which maintain plant  collections and plant records by a  professional staff and are open to the  public the study of native plants are  important to Botanical Gardens because  they serve missions including displaying  biodiversity beauty and history  so when I began this study I started  questioning why native plants were  increasing in use and one of my  observation was that they were being  used to address environmental issues  such as food for wildlife a reduction in  the use of chemical pesticides  fertilizers and water when planted in  their natural habitats preventing the  spread of alien invasive plants  contributing to fido remediation efforts  addressing climate change and increasing  ecosystem biodiversity they also are  valued for their aesthetic beauty and  regional sense of place my research  objectives for this study are to  understand how public gardens are using  native plants throughout their gardens  and program offerings what the  motivations are for doing so and if they  are addressing environmental issues  native plants are supported by research  but I wanted to know what motivates  public gardens for using them so I chose  for us botanical gardens based on  preliminary interviews their prominence  in the field and their collections  policies I conducted 223 phone  interviews to maintain consistency with  leadership staff of each garden  including the executive director  director of horticulture and director of  education when applicable these  interviews were followed up by site  visits when I had previously if I had  previously not visited the garden and I  conducted follow-up interviews based on  the need in the writing process so now  I'm just going to go through each site  first night was the lady bird johnson  wildflower center which is located in  austin texas and it was created through  the vision of Lady Bird Johnson and  actress Helen Hayes their mission serves  to focus their displays in education  around native plants of the state of  Texas  the second site is New England  wildflower society's living museum  garden in the woods it was founded and  designed by will Curtis and they display  native plants of the New England region  education at Garden in the woods centers  around engaging visitors to gain an  interest in using native plants the  third site was the bledel reserve  located on Bainbridge Island Washington  it was created through the inspiration  of prentice bloedel and his wife with  the mission of providing a tranquil  visitor experience this series of  landscape spans over 150 acres and is  picturesque of the Pacific Northwest the  final site was the Birmingham Botanical  Gardens which is unique to this study  and it offers a variety of collections  for visitors to experience including two  gardens of nature which focus on the  display and education of native plants  the collections were made possible by  donations of money and volunteer hours  by key individuals so now I'll tell you  a little bit about what I found in the  form of key themes differences  challenges successes and practical  implications from the study  so the first theme was the use of native  plants throughout the gardens and  program areas as pictured here native  plants permeate throughout the education  and displays at the wildflower center  and at Garden in the woods while their  styles vary at both of these gardens  teaching people to understand appreciate  and use the regional native flora is in  a sustainable manner is central to the  organizational goals the use of native  plants that the bledel reserve is  focused on providing a tranquil  experience currently they do not have a  formal education program at the  birmingham botanical gardens native  plants are displayed in two areas  focusing on the biodiversity the state  has to offer the Barbara Alabama  Woodlands displays mainly native trees  and serves as a hub of native plant  education for children the call  wildflower garden was developed from an  old stone quarry from the works progress  progress administration and is pictured  here and displays our basis native  plants placing a special focus on those  of wild provenance in Alabama while  native plants are not central to their  mission at the Birmingham Botanical  Gardens they do offer educational  programming for all ages that encourages  a deeper engagement with native plants a  second theme was the motivation behind  the use of native plants key figures  played a role in establishing each  garden or collection within the garden  in each case of this study beyond that  the key figures Lady Bird Johnson will  Curtis and Prentiss bledel respectively  at the wildflower center garden in the  woods and the bloedel reserve were all  influential in establishing a mission  that was instrumental in determining the  plant collections policies at these  gardens  a third theme was the motivation for  using native plants for environmental  purposes each garden in this study  emphasized using native plants for a  conservation purpose thus promoting an  awareness and in the case of why the  Wildflower Center and garden in the  woods the use and further conservation  of native plants garden in the woods  further has a display of rare and  endangered native plants gardens also  display native plants in order to  enhance their natural world so as to  bridge visitors to it a couple of  examples are public gardens providing a  place for visitors to see the effects of  climate change on plants and to see  solutions on how to address this issue  in the same way they are using native  plants and their natural habitats to  illustrate the reduction of water and  chemical inputs  some collections at the Wildflower  Center and guard in the woods  specifically were established to address  environmental issues through their  research efforts research areas included  native plant propagation carbon  sequestration with native grasses of  Texas green roof composition alternative  turf mixes water runoff and others in  the future each garden in the study is  increasing efforts to reduce their  environmental impact another trend that  arose in this study was the focus of  reaching three specific audience groups  professionals in the Lance in landscape  architecture architecture design and  contracting young families and singles  in their 20s and 30s by creating various  educational programs workshops and  garden events towards these different  groups they hope to garner the interest  and further their missions  there were two key differences between  the gardens in this study the first  difference was the specific  environmental issues that were addressed  because each garden resides in a  different climate zone in the u.s. each  garden focuses on a different issue for  example the blue del Reserve in  Washington State enjoys regular rainfall  however they are continually focused on  maintaining clean water sources the  Wildflower Center in Central Texas  places a major focus on water  conservation as they constantly face  drought another major difference was in  the educational programs offered the  blue del reserve was the only garden in  the study lacking a formal education  program although they do offer informal  programs such as walks and tours  education is completely focused on  native plants at the Wildflower Center  and garden in the woods and recently the  Birmingham Botanical Gardens joined in  efforts as all three of these gardens  offer a native plant certificate program  these certificates are awarded to  participants through their completion of  required and elective courses field  trips and volunteer service  each garden also experience exceptional  success in various ways of using native  plants the three pictured here are ways  that gardens are disseminating  information on native plants the native  plant database on the wildflower  Center's website is internationally  access and in last year alone received 5  million hits the recently launched  certificate in native plant studies at  the Birmingham Botanical Gardens has had  an overwhelming success in that their  response rate and that they have had to  create more classroom space to meet the  demand and they have enjoyed an  unexpected revenue generated the recent  launch of new england wildflower  society's go botany program a computer  but is a computer based program that is  used to facilitate education and  displays in a variety of settings and  according to staff is in high demand  success of the bloedel reserve comes in  the form of grant funding for  restoration programs which have  continually increased over the past two  and a half years staff members also know  that an anecdotal feedback from visitors  the key each garden also faces  challenges that affect their displays in  educational efforts of native plants one  of these is managing invasive plants  today 1.4 trillion dollars or five  percent of the global economy is spent  managing invasive species while  historically public gardens may have  played a role in bringing invasive  plants in through plant exploration they  are recognizing their damaging effects  and costs associated with them are  working to prevent their future  developments other challenges included  caring for a large space with limited  staff and budgets and overcoming the  general lack of knowledge of the value  and importance of native plants yes yes  that's a pretty picture to look at thank  you for pointing that up using native  plants in public gardens as presented in  this study culminates into a group of  practical implications these are  intended to build the success of other  gardens use of native plants and how  they use them to address environmental  issues these include establishing  expected outcomes in their use using  them in a way that continues the design  of the garden using their connection  with native with animals to bring in  younger audiences using them throughout  the collection instead of in individual  collections considering the  environmental effects of the design  process and to look at other  organizations for guides as creating  successful displays and educational  programs  I conducted this study this is to  discover how public gardens are using  native plants and if a main motivation  is to address environmental issues I  found that these gardens are using  native plants in diverse ways to not  only address environmental issues but  also to demonstrate that their value  goes beyond their positive environmental  roles these gardens are able to display  their beauty encouraging and  understanding and appreciation of native  plants and thus furthering their use so  I'd like to acknowledge my committee and  Cornell plantations for funding and  support throughout the process and the  other fellows in the program  yes places you visited express a  challenge of sourcing their native  plants which is propagating them or  finding the flyer and how do they  address that  um no they didn't because all gardens  have their own nurseries that it it  really didn't come up but I think  they're all some of them are wild  collecting seeds and I know it didn't  come up in the interviews at all  actually I think they're trying to  actually provide sources for people in  their communities that do have  challenges with that  yes done both bloedel reserve and the  Birmingham Botanical Gardens where it's  a mixture of new plant explains and  non-natives are the horticulturist  taking measures in the non-native  displays to make sure that they're not  introducing in vases that could migrate  into the native displays  I think that where that comes up is more  at the Birmingham Botanical Gardens the  barber Alabama woodlands that I  mentioned is probably 20 feet from their  Japanese garden and the Japanese garden  does have invasive plants so they are  constantly trying to pull those out and  that is something that they just  recognized as a challenge and it's  something that they have to do at the  Bedell reserve because everything grows  so fast invasives are always a major  challenge and the restoration programs  that they are implementing are really  helping to address that does that answer  your question yeah I know  did you get any response from the  um I think they are they're not sensing  the pressure but they are wanting to  reach out the groups so these  certificate programs that three of the  gardens have they are really trying to  reach people in the industries like  landscape architecture the to encourage  green building practices and establish  between plants and the correct habitats  so I I think it's the pressure is more  than reaching out to people and not  they're not getting that from people  outside that makes sense yes  so I think a lot of the gardens in the  study have seen the growth but they  haven't specifically been tracking it  and that is something that I recommend  for future research to really see what  the impacts are in numbers  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at cornell edu

